package com.hpcai270

// Days 152–168: InfiniBand, RDMA, RoCE and high-speed fabric engineering.
val hpcDaysPart15 = listOf(
Day(152,"InfiniBand architecture","The HPC fabric from HCA to switch","InfiniBand is a purpose-built high-performance interconnect. Learn HCAs, ports, links, switches, subnet management, queue pairs and the fabric control plane.","PATH\n\nProcess → RDMA verbs → HCA → InfiniBand link → switch fabric → destination HCA → memory.\n\nStorage can use the same fabric as compute or a dedicated storage fabric depending on architecture.","COMPONENTS\n\nHCA, switch, subnet manager, GUID, LID, port, link state. Understand what is control plane and what is data plane.","AI\n\nCollectives and storage benefit from predictable low-latency, high-bandwidth paths. The fabric must be engineered as a system.","LAB\n\nDraw a 4-node leaf-spine InfiniBand fabric. Label HCA ports, switches and management/subnet-manager roles.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can an AI cluster need a separate storage fabric? Discuss isolation, predictable bandwidth and failure-domain control.","GOAL\n\nSee InfiniBand as an engineered fabric, not simply a faster Ethernet cable."),
Day(153,"RDMA fundamentals","Remote memory access without normal CPU copy paths","RDMA allows an application to move data between hosts using network adapters with reduced CPU involvement compared with traditional socket I/O.","CONCEPTS\n\nQueue Pair, Completion Queue, Work Request, Work Completion, Memory Region, protection domain. Learn the vocabulary before tools.","OPERATIONS\n\nMemory must be registered and permissions matter. RDMA is fast because it moves work closer to the NIC, not because physics disappears.","LATENCY\n\nCPU overhead, PCIe topology, NIC processing, switch hops and congestion all contribute. ‘RDMA’ is not a single latency number.","LAB\n\nUse perftest or equivalent in a controlled environment when available. Compare message size and bandwidth/latency behavior.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain the difference between RDMA read/write and send/receive at a conceptual level.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand what RDMA changes in the data path."),
Day(154,"Queue pairs and completion queues","How RDMA work is actually submitted","Queue pairs contain send and receive or read/write work queues. Completion queues report finished operations. These structures are central to RDMA performance.","MODEL\n\nApplication posts work request → HCA executes → remote memory operation/message → completion generated → application polls/handles completion.","SCALING\n\nToo few queues can limit concurrency; too many can consume resources. Queue affinity and CPU/NIC locality matter.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nA connection may exist while application throughput remains low because of queue depth, message size, CPU polling or congestion.","LAB\n\nCreate a diagram showing 8 worker threads and 8 QPs feeding one HCA. Explain how concurrency changes throughput.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is a QP and why does an HPC storage engineer need to understand it?", "GOAL\n\nMove from ‘RDMA is fast’ to understanding the mechanism."),
Day(155,"RoCEv2","RDMA over Ethernet","RoCEv2 carries RDMA traffic over Ethernet/IP. Unlike InfiniBand, the Ethernet fabric must be engineered to behave well under loss and congestion.","STACK\n\nApplication → RDMA verbs → NIC → RoCEv2/UDP/IP → Ethernet → switch → destination NIC.","FABRIC\n\nLossless or low-loss behavior, ECN, PFC, QoS and correct MTU/configuration are important depending on design. Misconfiguration can produce severe performance problems.","TRADE-OFF\n\nRoCE can reuse high-speed Ethernet infrastructure and skills, but achieving predictable RDMA performance requires disciplined configuration.","LAB\n\nCompare an Ethernet TCP path with a RoCE conceptual path. List what additional fabric controls RDMA introduces.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is PFC not a universal cure for congestion? Discuss head-of-line blocking, pause propagation and the role of ECN.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand RoCE as a system-level networking discipline."),
Day(156,"Congestion control","Why fast networks still become slow","At high scale, congestion is often the performance limiter. AI clusters can create synchronized traffic bursts from collectives, checkpoints and dataset reads.","MODEL\n\nMany senders → shared link → queue grows → latency rises → packet drops or pause/ECN behavior → throughput changes.","SIGNALS\n\nECN marks, interface drops, pause counters, queue occupancy, retransmissions, application latency and throughput imbalance.","AI\n\nA synchronized training workload can create incast: many nodes send toward fewer destinations. Storage reads can create similar hotspots.","LAB\n\nDraw a 16-to-1 incast scenario. Identify where congestion forms and what metrics would prove it.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain why a 400/800 Gb/s link can still deliver poor application throughput. Link speed is capacity, not guaranteed end-to-end performance.","GOAL\n\nDiagnose congestion from evidence."),
Day(157,"PFC and ECN","RoCE control mechanisms","Priority Flow Control can pause selected traffic classes; Explicit Congestion Notification marks packets so senders can reduce traffic. They solve different parts of congestion behavior.","PFC\n\nReceiver/switch congestion can propagate pause upstream. This can protect a traffic class but may create head-of-line or pause storms if poorly designed.","ECN\n\nSwitch marks congestion; endpoints react according to congestion-control algorithms. It can provide feedback without relying solely on link-level pause.","DESIGN\n\nQoS class → DSCP/priority → switch queues → PFC/ECN policy → endpoint behavior. Consistency across the path matters.","LAB\n\nCreate a fabric policy diagram and list what must match between NICs and switches.","INTERVIEW\n\nIf a RoCE fabric has increasing PFC pause time and falling application throughput, what hypotheses do you test first?", "GOAL\n\nTreat QoS as an end-to-end contract."),
Day(158,"InfiniBand subnet management","How the fabric becomes usable","The subnet manager discovers and configures the InfiniBand fabric, assigning addressing and routes needed for communication.","CONTROL PLANE\n\nDiscovery → topology knowledge → addressing → routing → link activation.\n\nData plane traffic then uses the established fabric.","OPERATIONS\n\nUnderstand manager health, fabric state, link state and topology. A link can be physically up while the fabric is not correctly configured.","TOOLS\n\nibstat, ibv_devinfo, iblinkinfo, ibnetdiscover and vendor tools are common starting points; exact availability depends on stack.","LAB\n\nStudy sample topology output and translate it into a physical diagram.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat happens if the subnet manager is unavailable? Distinguish existing data paths from new/reconfigured fabric behavior.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand the control plane that makes IB forwarding possible."),
Day(159,"Fabric topology","Leaf-spine, fat-tree and oversubscription","Topology determines bisection bandwidth, path diversity and failure behavior. HPC systems often favor balanced fabrics because synchronized workloads expose bottlenecks quickly.","FAT-TREE\n\nLeaf/edge switches connect nodes; spine/core layers provide many equal-cost paths. A balanced design minimizes oversubscription.","OVERSUBSCRIPTION\n\nIf many downlinks share a smaller uplink capacity, the ratio predicts potential contention. The workload determines whether the theoretical bottleneck becomes real.","AI\n\nCollectives can stress east-west bandwidth. Storage traffic adds another dimension unless fabrics are separated.","LAB\n\nCalculate oversubscription for a simple 32-node leaf with 32×400G downlinks and 8×400G uplinks.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can a non-blocking fabric matter more for AI than for ordinary enterprise traffic? Discuss synchronized east-west communication.","GOAL\n\nDesign fabrics from traffic patterns, not port counts alone."),
Day(160,"NIC/HCA topology and GPU locality","PCIe and fabric locality","High-speed networking performance depends on which PCIe root complex and CPU socket owns a NIC and which GPU is connected to which fabric rail.","TOPOLOGY\n\nCPU socket ↔ PCIe root ↔ NIC/HCA ↔ switch fabric; GPU ↔ PCIe/NVLink. The shortest path often matters.","TOOLS\n\n`lspci -tv`, `numactl`, `nvidia-smi topo -m`, vendor topology tools. Map device IDs to physical topology.","AI\n\nA GPU reading from a remote NUMA NIC may consume additional PCIe/inter-socket resources. Rail-optimized designs aim to balance these paths.","LAB\n\nDraw a dual-socket 8-GPU node and label two NICs. Decide which NIC each GPU should prefer and explain why.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can identical NICs have different application performance depending on placement?", "GOAL\n\nInclude PCIe topology in performance reasoning."),
Day(161,"Network benchmarking","iperf is not enough","Network testing must match the transport and workload. TCP bandwidth, RDMA bandwidth, latency and application traffic answer different questions.","TEST LAYERS\n\nTCP/UDP baseline → RDMA microbenchmark → filesystem/application benchmark.\n\nEach layer can reveal a different bottleneck.","VARIABLES\n\nmessage size, concurrency, streams, direction, CPU affinity, MTU, topology and traffic class.","LAB\n\nDesign a benchmark matrix for 2, 8 and 32 nodes. Record aggregate and per-node throughput and tail latency.","INTERVIEW\n\nA NIC reports 400 Gb/s but application throughput is 250 Gb/s. Explain what evidence you collect before calling the NIC faulty.","GOAL\n\nBenchmark the layer where the claim exists."),
Day(162,"RDMA troubleshooting","A disciplined fault tree","RDMA failures often span hardware, firmware, driver, kernel, userspace libraries and fabric configuration.","FAULT TREE\n\nPCIe device → driver → firmware → RDMA device → link state → addressing/path → QP setup → application operation → performance.","TOOLS\n\n`rdma link`, `ibstat`, `ibv_devinfo`, `ethtool`, `dmesg`, vendor firmware tools, switch telemetry and perftest.","SYMPTOMS\n\nNo device, link down, connection failure, low bandwidth, high latency, intermittent completion errors. Map each to a layer.","LAB\n\nCreate a troubleshooting matrix with evidence for five RDMA symptoms. Include what not to change blindly.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow do you distinguish a fabric problem from an application/QP problem? State the measurements you would compare.","GOAL\n\nTroubleshoot RDMA from bottom to top."),
Day(163,"NVMe over Fabrics","Remote NVMe command path","NVMe-oF extends the NVMe command model to remote storage. Common transports include RDMA and TCP; the transport changes latency and infrastructure requirements.","PATH\n\nApplication → filesystem/block layer → NVMe host → NVMe-oF transport → fabric → NVMe target → SSDs.","RDMA VERSION\n\nCan reduce software/network overhead and is common in high-performance designs. TCP offers broader Ethernet compatibility.","AI\n\nNVMe-oF can expose high-performance remote flash to compute, but distributed filesystems are often used when many clients need shared POSIX semantics.","LAB\n\nCompare local NVMe, NVMe-oF/RDMA and NVMe-oF/TCP in a design table: latency, CPU overhead, scale and operational complexity.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhen would you choose NVMe-oF instead of a parallel filesystem? Discuss block semantics versus shared-file semantics.","GOAL\n\nChoose storage protocol from application semantics."),
Day(164,"Fabric isolation","Compute, storage and management networks","Large AI systems often separate traffic classes or fabrics to protect performance and operations.","EXAMPLE\n\nCompute fabric: GPU-to-GPU. Storage fabric: GPU/node-to-storage. Management: provisioning/monitoring. OOB: BMC/switch management.","WHY\n\nA checkpoint burst should not prevent administrators from reaching nodes. Conversely, management traffic should not consume scarce storage bandwidth.","SECURITY\n\nIsolation reduces blast radius and simplifies policy, but increases infrastructure and operational complexity.","LAB\n\nDesign four network planes for a 128-GPU cluster and specify interfaces, switches and failure domains.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhen is separate physical storage fabric worth the cost? Answer from workload isolation, bandwidth and reliability requirements.","GOAL\n\nTreat network planes as architecture decisions."),
Day(165,"NVIDIA networking ecosystem","ConnectX, Spectrum, Quantum and software","Modern NVIDIA AI infrastructure combines high-speed NICs/DPUs, Ethernet or InfiniBand switches and software for fabric management and telemetry.","REFERENCE\n\nConnectX-class adapters provide high-speed networking/RDMA. Quantum families serve InfiniBand fabrics; Spectrum families serve Ethernet AI fabrics. Exact model capabilities depend on generation.","SOFTWARE\n\nUFM and related tooling can provide fabric management/visibility in supported environments. Network Operator integrates networking into Kubernetes environments.","AI\n\nDo not memorize product names without understanding the traffic path: GPU/node → NIC → switch → NIC → destination.","LAB\n\nBuild a vendor-neutral component map and then annotate where NVIDIA products could fit.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is the difference between a NIC/HCA, DPU and switch? Explain their roles in the data path.","GOAL\n\nBe able to discuss vendor hardware without becoming vendor-dependent."),
Day(166,"Ethernet versus InfiniBand","Choosing the fabric","Both can deliver high performance. The correct decision depends on ecosystem, operational skills, workload, topology, congestion requirements and application support.","COMPARE\n\nInfiniBand: purpose-built HPC fabric, strong ecosystem for AI/HPC. Ethernet/RoCE: Ethernet ecosystem with RDMA capability. Neither should be selected from headline bandwidth alone.","AI\n\nCollective-heavy training, storage access and multi-tenant traffic place different demands on the fabric.","OPERATIONS\n\nEvaluate monitoring, automation, spare parts, skills, cabling, support and existing standards.","LAB\n\nWrite a decision matrix for a new 256-GPU cluster. Give weighted scores and document assumptions.","INTERVIEW\n\nIf a company already has a mature 400G Ethernet team, why might RoCE be attractive? What would still need to be proven?"," +
        ""GOAL\n\nDefend a fabric decision with evidence and operational realism."),
Day(167,"Network failure domains","Cables, optics, ports, switches and paths","High-speed fabrics fail in many ways: optic, cable, port, switch, line card, power domain, routing and configuration. Resilience must be visible in the topology.","DESIGN\n\nDual rails → independent switch paths → redundant power → separate management. Avoid single shared components hidden behind ‘redundant’ labels.","OPERATIONS\n\nTrack link errors, flaps, CRC, optical levels, lane issues, congestion and firmware compatibility.","LAB\n\nTake a 16-node topology and mark what happens if one leaf switch, one uplink, one NIC and one cable fail.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is the difference between link redundancy and application-level resilience? Explain using storage traffic.","GOAL\n\nDesign for component failure without guessing."),
Day(168,"High-speed networking checkpoint","Whiteboard the fabric","You should now be able to explain TCP/IP basics, RDMA, RoCEv2, InfiniBand, topology, congestion, QoS, NVMe-oF and troubleshooting.","WHITEBOARD\n\nGPU → PCIe → HCA/NIC → leaf → spine → leaf → HCA/NIC → storage or GPU. Add management and monitoring paths.","QUESTIONS\n\nQP? CQ? ECN? PFC? MTU? oversubscription? bisection bandwidth? NUMA locality? rail optimization? NVMe-oF?"," +
        ""SCENARIO\n\nStorage throughput drops during a large training checkpoint. Prove or disprove congestion, storage backend saturation, client queueing and application synchronization.","LAB\n\nWrite a complete network incident runbook from physical layer to application. Include evidence, safe tests and rollback.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain why network engineering is now a core storage skill in AI infrastructure.","STANDARD\n\nYou should be able to sit with a network engineer and discuss the same evidence without hiding behind storage terminology.")
)
